checkInstructions.visualCheck:
Criterion 1 is the demo. Run, from the repo root:

  scripts/agent/jet-env cargo build
  scripts/agent/jet-env jet fmt --check --simplify --diff examples

That prints every rewrite --simplify would make across the example corpus.
Three representative ones, from real corpus files:

R1  examples/features/errors/qq_panic.jet
    before: fn maybe() => Int? { return None }
    after:  fn maybe() => Int? :: None

R3  examples/features/basics/named_args.jet
    before: fn new(width: Float, height: Float) => Rect { return Rect.{width: width, height: height} }
    after:  fn new(width: Float, height: Float) => Rect { return .{width: width, height: height} }

R2  a chained classic if becomes the ordered arm table L0507 already asks for
    before:  if score >= 90 {
                 return "a"
             } else if score >= 80 {
                 return "b"
             } else {
                 return "c"
             }
    after:   if {
                 score >= 90 -> { return "a" }
                 score >= 80 -> { return "b" }
                 else -> { return "c" }
             }

Good looks like: each rewrite is the spelling you would have asked for, the
meaning is unchanged, and nothing gets harder to read.

The catalog is opt-in per rewrite, so bounce with the letter (R1, R2, or R3)
you reject and the rest can still ship.

