Option A explicitly selects first-party implementation using existing runtime IO. It is a proposal, not presumed authorization.

## Exact independent API choice

```text
serve(app: App, address: String, path: String, transports: [String], event: String, action: String, acknowledgement: String) Void !TransportError
```

The fixture explicitly selects transports=[websocket] and acknowledgement=result; unsupported selections fail Config before listening.

TransportError distinguishes Config, Listen and Protocol using secret-safe messages.

Socket.IO event order maps to the existing typed record-order action.

The acknowledgement contains that action result, not a native WebSocket frame acknowledgement.

Connection-owned authority and abort state are released on disconnect.

Reconnect creates a fresh connection owner, not a second business identity.

There is no automatic resend or durable delivery promise.

The client may resubmit its original idempotency key; the shared ledger returns the same receipt.

A lost acknowledgement leaves delivery outcome unknown. #3098 owns the public binding; the separate Core-owned websocket-lifetime card retains cleanup and cancellation proof.

